# AI research instructions for Erdős Problem #99

This repository is a theorem ledger plus a large provenance archive. The two layers must
not be conflated.
 

## Permanent agent-only mathematical review policy

This project is researched, audited, and mathematically governed by autonomous AI
agents. Do not seek human mathematicians, human peer review, or human sign-off for a
proof or theorem promotion. Consequential canonical promotion requires **one independent AI-agent mathematical
audit** of the exact candidate SHA. The reviewing agent independently reconstructs
the full proof **and** adversarially checks its decisive bridge and regressions.
The project owner may operate this independent agent to review other agents' work;
no second review, different model family, or human proof referee is required.
The proposing agent or its own checker cannot count as this independent review.
Record agent/model identities when available, evidence, scope, and verdicts; leave
unresolved claims noncanonical. See `docs/REVIEW_POLICY.md`.

## Mandatory bootstrap

Before doing new mathematics:

1. read `docs/STATE.md`;
2. read `docs/state/theorem-state.yaml` when lifecycle/status ambiguity matters;
3. read `docs/problem/statement.md` for the exact global quantifiers;
4. read the route map for the chosen target;
5. read every active theorem module explicitly named by that route map;
6. read only the shared theory modules the argument actually uses;
7. read the current route prompt from `docs/prompts/README.md` last.

Do not start by searching all reports. A repository-wide search can return rejected or
superseded statements before the canonical theorem.

## Authority hierarchy

When two sources disagree:

1. `docs/STATE.md` and `docs/state/theorem-state.yaml` control lifecycle/status;
2. the active target route map and named active modules control theorem content;
3. reports explicitly promoted by the state lock or route map are canonical for the
   exact statements promoted;
4. audited-working reports are research leads, not unqualified premises;
5. conditional reports and regressions follow;
6. unreviewed research artifacts are discovery material only;
7. superseded, rejected, historical-prompt, and archive material is provenance only.

The September 11 repository consolidation was a provenance merge. A file being on
`main`, a merged PR, a high section number, or a recent date does not promote its claims.

## Mission and Level I criterion

For unit-separated planar sets let `D(n)` be the minimum possible diameter of an
`n`-point configuration. A Level I proof must show that for all sufficiently large `n`,
every global minimizer contains a unit equilateral triangle. A Level I disproof must
produce triangle-free global minimizers for an unbounded sequence of cardinalities.

Local minima, finite examples, asymptotically dense packings, one good minimizer per
cardinality, or a lower-dimensional/shell-only regression do not settle those
quantifiers.

**Current Level I status: open.**

## Current Target A invariant

**Status: open.**

Authority: `docs/targets/target-a/README.md`.

The canonical chain runs through Section 177. The Section-167 source-labeled planar
Menger lemma and Section-174 full basis-closed shield charge are historical
intermediate frontiers, not the current endpoint.

The global closure remains

`rank S <= d_int + |C^partial| - Delta_Lambda`,

equivalently `d_sc >= Delta_Lambda`.

The preferred locked-response endpoint is the Section-175 interior-tube
right-deficiency inequality. Section 176 bounds the zero-slack inner-source defect and
makes saturating matchings sector-local outside an explicit boundary-order exception.
Section 177 adds edge-lens/coarse-Hall and source-gate refinements; neither branch is
closed. Fixed-source same-hull Hall capacity is already exact, so a valid closure must
add information through the actual response, a source-specific sector inequality, or a
strict diameter improvement.

Do not infer a Schur kernel from planarity, local cages, many candidate cavities, or a
single Hall circuit without a simultaneous/global coupling theorem.

## Current Target B invariant

**Status: open.**

Authority: `docs/targets/target-b/README.md`.

The exact sufficient closure is the joint outer-shell stability inequality

`G+E >= (sqrt(3)/4) B + (sqrt(3)/2)(t+chi-1)`.

The coherent near-extremal core has been excluded under the promoted phase hypotheses.
The remaining obstruction is global: multi-grain transfer, fixed quadratic phase
deficit, and topology/boundary corrections cannot be discarded.

Later dense-grid/penetration/phase-interpolation reports are audited-working unless the
state lock explicitly promotes them.

## Current Target C invariant

Authority: `docs/targets/target-c/README.md` and
`docs/targets/target-c/CURRENT.md`.

**C-I is closed. C-II is open.** Do not spend a research run trying to prove universal
selected-stress PSD or eliminate the C-I shear separator; the September 9 orientation
audit restored C53/C63 and closed that gate.

For C-II use the contact-minimal, then diameter-pair-minimal source with `C=C_*` and
`H=H_*`, retaining all contact-isolated source points.

Current canonical additional reductions are:

- the zero/additive response-transfer branch is closed: `min_{e!=f} K_ef<0` for the
  promoted regime, with no uniform negative gap claimed;
- the full loaded diameter graph has exact Woodall topology: `d_H=0` gives one
  odd-unicyclic graph with pendant leaves; `d_H>0` gives exactly `d_H` caterpillar
  components;
- all selected diameter routes have one common positive-contact bridge block;
- the universal common-contact-bridge phase is impossible.

The independent PR #188 audit promotes the scoped all-phase renormalized-surplus theorem above `0.9604`; the independent PR #191 audit rejects its former `0.995540...` ordinary-surplus theorem while preserving the scoped cyclic structural facts. Use the generated theorem graph and current Target C ledger rather than older report labels.

For the live C-II obligations, follow `docs/targets/target-c/CURRENT.md`: the full-core
branch still needs a source-realized face-coupled finite move or discrete replacement
that passes every source lower constraint, actual-diameter upper constraint, and unary
rhombus test, then gives strict contact loss at diameter `D` or a strict sub-`D`
competitor. The separate macroscopic-container branch still lacks an arbitrary-container
packet-capacity or moved-core strict-diameter closure. Audit.230's quarter-fold isolate
population count does not supply an integrated endpoint or capacity under moved geometry.
Retain every source point, including contact-isolated points.

## Binding regressions and corrections

Before claiming a general theorem, check the route-specific regression list and at
least the following repository-wide failures when relevant:

- local/jammed behavior is not global minimum-diameter optimality;
- source configurations, smaller optima, lattice scaffolds, and response networks are
  distinct objects unless a theorem co-embeds them;
- many one-at-a-time feasible exchanges need not be simultaneously compatible;
- boundary-order losses can destroy exact one-integer contradictions;
- generic matrix rank or PSD does not automatically give the source-specific Schur
  nullity or finite motion needed;
- a universally zero KKT multiplier need not integrate to a finite release without the
  relevant shear/nonlinear theorem;
- consecutive incident diameter states do not imply an Euler trail/path; a star is a
  counterexample;
- the force-bulk FB4 localization is rejected;
- the old incompatible-orientation pentagon regression against C53 is rejected;
- Rogalski–Saint Raymond equality uniqueness is not supplied by the cited 1985 source;
- the late five-arc Eppstein near-quadrangulation interpretation is rejected even though
  its branch retained the associated working scalar bound;
- contact-isolated source points may not be dropped from Target C source-population
  arguments.

## Run discipline

At the start of a serious run, record the repository commit, state version, chosen
target, exact theorem being attacked, and proof dependencies. If the repository changes
during the run, compare the state files before importing anything new.

Before investing in a candidate lemma, write

`candidate lemma -> exact canonical quantity -> contradiction/Level I consequence`.

If that implication chain is unavailable, the idea is exploratory.

Trust canonical inputs by default and audit the new bridge. Reopen old results only when
a new argument exposes a concrete hypothesis issue, an exact regression conflicts with
them, or the state lock records a later correction.

## Branch hygiene

Agents that create working branches own their cleanup. Once a branch's work is merged,
explicitly superseded, or intentionally abandoned, delete that branch promptly after
verifying that it contains no unique unmerged research that still needs preservation.
A run is not operationally complete while it leaves behind stale aliases, obsolete
predecessor branches, or temporary maintenance branches.

Keep a branch only while an open PR or active run still depends on it, or while it
contains unique unmerged work that has not yet been preserved elsewhere. A branch is
not a provenance mechanism by itself: merged commits, PR history, reports, and Git
history preserve provenance. Never delete another active branch merely because it is
old; compare it against `main` and the relevant PRs first. Temporary workflows or other
cleanup-only files created solely to remove branches must stay off `main` and disappear
with their maintenance branch.

## Claim classification

Every substantial output must classify its new claims as one of:

- proved candidate awaiting promotion;
- unreviewed candidate;
- audited-working;
- conditional;
- regression;
- rejected.

Only the promotion procedure can make a result canonical. Changing prose from
“candidate” to “theorem” is not promotion.

## Computation

Use scripts for exact finite reductions, falsification, enumeration, and certified
arithmetic. A numerical search can kill a universal finite claim when the resulting
counterexample is exact/certified. It cannot establish global optimality unless a
separate theorem proves that implication.

Document what each script proves, what it assumes, and what it does not prove in
`research/README.md`.

## Literature

Import theorem statements only after checking the original or a reliable transcription,
with exact hypotheses, normalization, and applicability. Record near misses rather than
forcing them into the proof.

A cleanup-time example: the Woodall straight-line-thrackle classification was rechecked
before promotion. By contrast, the earlier use of Rogalski–Saint Raymond equality
uniqueness was rejected because the cited source does not prove uniqueness.

## Reports and prompts

Reports are noncanonical and `unreviewed` by default unless an explicit ledger entry records a completed audit or promotion. `docs/reports/README.md` explains the archive policy, and each indexed artifact carries a generated lifecycle header. Historical prompts are instructions from an earlier theorem state, not theorem
sources and not current task definitions.

Use the current prompts named by `docs/prompts/README.md`. Compatibility paths for the
old Target A and Target C prompts now redirect rather than silently presenting stale
missions.

## Promotion contract

A frontier-changing result must update, in one change:

1. increment `state_version` in `docs/state/theorem-state.yaml`;
2. `docs/STATE.md`;
3. the affected target route map or current-frontier file;
4. the active route prompt if the next obligation changes;
5. dependency edges and certificate registrations when applicable;
6. regenerated artifact metadata/index and generated state views.

Follow `docs/RESEARCH_WORKFLOW.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md`. Run
`python3 tools/check_theorem_state.py` before committing.

Never create another monolithic “canonical complete report” as a competing source of
truth. Preserve history in reports/archive; keep current theorem state small and
explicit.
